# cross_context_call_test.masm
#
# Exercises the caller / callee context isolation that Miden's
# `call.X` boundary establishes.  Unlike `exec.X` (inline expansion
# in the caller's memory context, no stack-depth normalisation),
# `call.X` is a true cross-context call that:
#
#   1. Allocates a fresh memory context (writes at `mem_store.N`
#      inside the callee land in the callee's distinct memory
#      space; they are not visible to the caller via the same
#      address after return).
#   2. Normalises the operand stack at the boundary: the callee
#      observes a 16-felt stack at entry whose top elements are
#      the caller-passed arguments (Miden's calling convention
#      for `call.X` truncates the stack to exactly 16 felts).
#
# Layout:
#
#   `proc.callee.1`  — declares one local slot (`loc_store.0`),
#                      writes `loc_store.0` and `mem_store.10`
#                      to demonstrate the callee has its own
#                      FMP-relative local frame and its own
#                      memory context.  Returns a known constant
#                      (777) on top of the stack so the caller
#                      can verify the cross-context return.
#
#   `begin`         — `push.123 push.456 mem_store.10` writes
#                      a marker into the CALLER's memory at
#                      address 10, then `call.callee` invokes
#                      the callee in a fresh context.  After
#                      return, the caller can observe stack[0]
#                      == 777 (callee's return value), proving
#                      the cross-context call returned a value
#                      via the operand stack.
#
# Strict pin (matching test): the callee's call_entry stages a
# truncated 4-felt stack snapshot (s0..s3 = the caller's top
# four felts), and the post-call step in `#main` carries the
# callee's return value (777) on stack[0].

proc.callee.1
    # Write a value to the callee's local slot 0 (FMP-relative).
    # The callee's FMP is fresh (Miden allocates a new local
    # frame for each `call.X` invocation), so this write does
    # not collide with any caller-side local state.
    push.555
    loc_store.0
    # Write to the callee's memory context at address 10.
    # The caller wrote 456 at the same address pre-call -- the
    # callee's write here lands in the callee's distinct memory
    # context, so the caller's value at address 10 remains 456
    # after the call returns.  (The recorder's memory variable
    # tracking does not yet surface this isolation directly --
    # see cross_context_call_test_via_ct_print_full's notes --
    # but the side-effect is observable via the FMP boundary.)
    push.999 push.10
    mem_store
    # Push the callee's return value (777) on top of the stack.
    # Miden's `call.X` calling convention truncates the operand
    # stack to exactly 16 felts at the boundary, so anything
    # below stack[15] is dropped.  The return value lives at
    # stack[0] when the callee returns.  We then drop stack[1]
    # so the net stack depth on return matches the call's
    # entry depth (16 -> 16).
    push.777
    swap drop
end

begin
    # Marker value written to the CALLER's memory context at
    # address 10.  After `call.callee` returns, this value
    # remains intact -- the callee's mem_store.10 lands in the
    # callee's distinct memory space.
    push.456 push.10
    mem_store
    # Leading push so the recorder synthesises a #main call frame.
    push.123
    # The cross-context call.  Per Miden's calling convention
    # the operand stack is truncated to 16 felts at this
    # boundary; the callee sees the caller's top 16 felts as
    # its starting stack.  After return, the callee's return
    # value (777) is on stack[0] and the caller's prior top
    # (123) is on stack[1].
    call.callee
    # Drop the callee's return value and the caller's marker
    # so the program ends with a clean stack.
    drop drop
end
